\section{Count Ownership Edges, Not Variables in the Source}

For an immediate reference counter, an object's count is the
number of strong incoming edges, including owning root slots.
A register borrowed while a strong owner remains live is not an
additional edge. For example, reading a child from a list node
can yield a borrowed reference, but if calling an unknown helper
might overwrite the field or destroy the node, the reference
must be retained across that call. Appel describes the extra
operations on ordinary stores and the possibility of eliminating
some of them with dataflow analysis \cite{appel2004modern}.
The optimization requires a lifetime argument, not just algebra
showing that an increment and decrement would add to zero.

For a strong replacement store, retain the new value before
releasing the old one. In the self-assignment case the slot could
be the last owner; a decrement first might reclaim the object
before the increment. Publish the new slot value before invoking
any zero-count cleanup, so code observing the holder sees the
replacement. In a simple collector, process zero-count objects
through a queue: outgoing edges stay strong until removed by
that queue. A million-element chain then does not require a
million host stack frames. Budgeting the drain bounds the cost
of an individual drain slice, not the memory waiting in the
queue. The allocation path may still have to do substantial
work if the queue lags behind the mutator.

\section{Find Out Where Nonzero Counts Come From}

In \Cref{fig:visual-410}, the two nodes keep each other's count
positive even after the root is removed. If \(B\) has two
separate fields pointing to \(A\), subtract two edges in a
cycle test, not one. The graph used for classification is a
multigraph. If there is a root to \(B\), classification must
instead preserve both objects, including \(A\), whose own
count has no external contribution.

