\section{Classify Candidates With Trial Deletion}

\begin{verbatim}
replace_strong(slot, value):
    retain(value)
    previous := slot.load()
    slot.store(value)
    release(previous)

classify(candidates):
    # Stop the mutator; actual counts and edges are stable.
    for x in candidates:
        trial[x] := count[x]
    for x in candidates:
        for y in strong_targets(x):
            if y in candidates: trial[y] -= 1
    seeds := {x in candidates where trial[x] > 0}
    kept := reach_inside_candidates(seeds)
    return candidates minus kept
\end{verbatim}

The positive trial counts represent incoming edges from outside
the set, so seeds and their descendants inside the set must
survive \cite{jones2011garbage}. Every path from a root into the set starts with an
outside incoming edge, unless the root was omitted from the
true counts; that omission invalidates the premise. A candidate
set can be incomplete and still safe, but it may leave garbage
alive. If \(B\) is excluded, its edge to \(A\) looks external
and can preserve \(A\) even in the absence of any real root.
Building a candidate set by following strong edges from an
object whose count dropped but remained positive is one way to
reduce such missed opportunities. It does not guarantee a cheap
pass: the suspect can lead into much of the live heap.

The result must be reclaimed as a group. First mark its members
as doomed, while the graph remains stable. Remove their outgoing
edges to survivors exactly once, enqueueing ordinary zero-count
work as needed. Internal edges between doomed nodes need not
invoke ordinary release; both endpoints are about to disappear.
An implementation must not publish half of a freed cycle to
the allocator and then consult its old headers while freeing the
other half. Temporarily defer recursive destruction during the
group operation. This example assumes no resurrection or
user-defined finalizers; adding either requires a distinct
semantic phase rather than reusing ordinary zero-count code
blindly. Alternatively, take a full tracing pass occasionally
and reclaim unmarked objects as one unreachable set.

\section{Choose an Update Model and Test It}

Atomic increments and decrements alone do not let a thread
safely acquire a strong reference from a location another thread
can clear. Reading a raw address and then incrementing can race
with the last release. A shared slot must instead use a lock
or another acquisition protocol that keeps the object alive
until the retain has succeeded. Weak acquisition has the same
issue. In a count-plus-control-block design, weak owners keep
the control block, not the payload, alive. Upgrading attempts an
atomic transition from a nonzero strong count, without allowing
the zero state to become live again. Counter overflow also needs
a policy. Wrapping to zero is not one; checked overflow or a
deliberate immortal state preserves safety at different costs.

To test local optimizations, instrument owning stores and compute
the incoming degree independently at quiescent points, including
queued objects that still own fields. Compare the result to
stored counts. Use a diamond graph to exercise sharing, a
self-replacement with exactly one owner to exercise ordering, and
a dead cycle with a live outgoing tail to exercise boundary
decrements. In the last fixture an independent root holds the
tail. Its count should fall, not reach zero. Drop that root and
verify ordinary release reclaims it. Record zero-queue backlog
and candidate traversal size separately: a collector can have
cheap decrement instructions yet spend too long processing
their delayed consequences.

\section{Model Transfers and Reentrancy Explicitly}

Some calling conventions transfer an owned reference rather
than borrow it. In intermediate code, giving an owned value
to one successor is not the same as copying to both. Consider
a branch that sends a freshly returned value to one of two
blocks. Each block can take the original ownership token:
the executions are exclusive. Within one block, splitting the
value into two owning fields must create a second token. At a
control-flow join, the compiler must reconcile ownership on
every incoming edge, including unwind edges. A release hoisted
out of a branch is valid only when every execution reaching it
owns the reference it releases. This matters even if a test
never drives the count below zero: prematurely releasing while
a borrowed use remains is enough to make execution incorrect.

For an ordinary cache with a strong field and a notifier, plan
for callbacks that can reenter the cache. A replacement might
install \(Y\), release the last count of \(X\), and enter a
cleanup callback that replaces \(Y\) with \(Z\). The original
replacement must not assume its field still contains \(Y\)
when the callback returns; it also must not invoke the callback
under a nonreentrant cache lock. A common approach performs
the owning exchange under that lock and releases the old
owning reference after unlocking. If the API returns the new
value, it can return a separately held strong reference, not
a borrowed reread of the slot. Test notifications on two
nested replacements and check both release counts.

Deferred counting changes the model. An implementation may
keep no counts on certain temporary stack references and scan
those roots before reclaiming an object with zero heap count.
The zero value then denotes a candidate, not a verdict. Trial
deletion must include that independent root information or
operate only on a reconciled counting state. Mixing deferred
updates from one design with a reclamation condition from the
immediate design invalidates both proofs.
